From: conda-forge
Subject: [PATCH] python: don't release GIL during interpreter finalization

Python 3.15 tightened interpreter finalization and thread-state handling.
SWIG-generated destructors can invoke XapianSWIG_Python_Thread_Allow while
Python is already finalizing.  Calling PyEval_SaveThread() in that state
can result in a fatal error such as:

    PyMutex_Unlock: unlocking mutex that is not locked
    Python runtime state: finalizing

There is no benefit to releasing the GIL while deleting these wrapper
objects during interpreter shutdown, so keep the current thread state
attached once finalization has started.

Py_IsFinalizing() is public since Python 3.13.  Limit the workaround to
Python 3.15+, where the problem is observed.

Patch both the SWIG input and the generated wrapper, since release
tarballs build the pre-generated xapian_wrap.cc without requiring SWIG.

--- a/python3/python.i
+++ b/python3/python.i
@@ -107,6 +107,15 @@ class XapianSWIG_Python_Thread_Allow {
     bool status;
   public:
     XapianSWIG_Python_Thread_Allow() : status(true) {
+#if PY_VERSION_HEX >= 0x030f0000
+        // Don't detach the thread state once interpreter finalization has
+        // started.  In Python 3.15, doing so while SWIG proxy objects are
+        // being destroyed during shutdown can corrupt runtime lock state.
+        if (Py_IsFinalizing()) {
+            status = false;
+            return;
+        }
+#endif
 #if PY_VERSION_HEX < 0x03070000
         // Since 3.7, Python initialises the GIL in PyInitialize() so
         // PyEval_ThreadsInitialized() is no longer useful and was
--- a/python3/xapian_wrap.cc
+++ b/python3/xapian_wrap.cc
@@ -76,6 +76,15 @@ class XapianSWIG_Python_Thread_Allow {
     bool status;
   public:
     XapianSWIG_Python_Thread_Allow() : status(true) {
+#if PY_VERSION_HEX >= 0x030f0000
+        // Don't detach the thread state once interpreter finalization has
+        // started.  In Python 3.15, doing so while SWIG proxy objects are
+        // being destroyed during shutdown can corrupt runtime lock state.
+        if (Py_IsFinalizing()) {
+            status = false;
+            return;
+        }
+#endif
 #if PY_VERSION_HEX < 0x03070000
         // Since 3.7, Python initialises the GIL in PyInitialize() so
         // PyEval_ThreadsInitialized() is no longer useful and was
